  "worked_solution": "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274884/assets/72ec33b41b4a7d924cec49ca1d55856c36c56cec4db1e7c347eefc06cbe2d2f2.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<-\\frac{1}{4},Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1/4}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nHowever, $f(u,v)$ is zero everywhere inside the region $Δ.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1/4}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
